# Beacon Rev H Klipper Setup for VzBot 330

This guide documents a practical Beacon Rev H setup on a VzBot 330 running Klipper.

It assumes:

* Beacon Rev H is mounted on the toolhead.
* Beacon USB is connected directly to the Raspberry Pi.
* Klipper, Moonraker, and Mainsail are already installed.
* The printer has already been flashed and can connect to the MCU.
* A working `printer.cfg` already exists, or you are adapting one from a previous setup.

Important: Beacon warns against hot-plugging. Connect or disconnect Beacon USB only when the Raspberry Pi is powered off.

---

## 1. Install Beacon Klipper module

SSH into the Raspberry Pi:

```bash
ssh pi@vzbot330.local
```

Install Beacon Klipper:

```bash
cd ~
git clone https://github.com/beacon3d/beacon_klipper.git
./beacon_klipper/install.sh
```

During installation, Beacon may report that the firmware is already current.

Example:

```text
Beacon 'xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx' is already flashed with the current firmware version.
```

This means Beacon was detected by the installer.

---

## 2. Add Beacon to Moonraker update manager

Open `moonraker.conf`: directly in the the Mainsail Dashboard 

```browser
http://vzbot330.local/config
```

OR

```bash
nano ~/printer_data/config/moonraker.conf
```

Add: (in the end of the file)

```ini
# Beacon Probe Rev H update manager
[update_manager beacon]
type: git_repo
channel: dev
path: ~/beacon_klipper
origin: https://github.com/beacon3d/beacon_klipper.git
env: ~/klippy-env/bin/python
requirements: requirements.txt
install_script: install.sh
is_system_service: False
managed_services: klipper
info_tags: desc=Beacon Surface Scanner
```

Save the file and restart Moonraker if needed.

This allows Beacon to be updated from the web interface.

---

## 3. Find the Beacon serial path

Run:

```bash
ls /dev/serial/by-id/
```

or:

```bash
ls /dev/serial/by-id/*Beacon*
```

Copy the full Beacon serial path.

Example format:

```text
/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Beacon_Beacon_RevH_xxxxxxxxxxxx-if00
```

Do not copy another user’s serial path. Your Beacon serial will be different.

---

## 4. Add Beacon to printer.cfg

Open `printer.cfg` in Mainsail.

Add or verify:

```ini
[beacon]
serial: /dev/serial/by-id/usb-Beacon_Beacon_RevH_xxxxxxxxxxxx-if00
x_offset: 0
y_offset: 22.8
mesh_main_direction: x
mesh_runs: 2
```

Important:

* `serial` must match your own Beacon.
* `x_offset` and `y_offset` must match your toolhead.
* The values above are examples from this VzBot 330 setup.

Then SAVE & RESTART

---

Sometimes a PROBE error appears in the Dashboard so either check printer.cfg and SAVE & RESTART
If the error persist then reboot the Raspberry Pi:

```bash
sudo reboot
```

If it still persist then continue bellow to point 5. 

---

## 5. Beacon replaces [probe], not bed mesh or safe z home

If your config has an old physical probe section, remove it.

Remove old sections such as:

```ini
[probe]
```

or:

```ini
[bltouch]
```

Do not delete these sections just because they are under a comment heading called “Probe”:

```ini
[safe_z_home]
```

```ini
[bed_mesh]
```

```ini
[resonance_tester]
```

Beacon still needs a valid `[bed_mesh]` section.

---

## 6. Z endstop configuration

For Beacon homing, the Z stepper should use Beacon as the virtual endstop.

In your `[stepper_z]` section, use:

```ini
endstop_pin: probe:z_virtual_endstop
homing_retract_dist: 0
```

Important: Beacon needs `homing_retract_dist: 0`.

---

## 7. Safe Z home

Set the safe Z home position near the center of the bed.

For this VzBot 330 setup:

```ini
[safe_z_home]
home_xy_position: 159.5, 151.5
z_hop: 3
```

Adjust this for your own machine.

---

## 8. Bed mesh configuration

Beacon requires a valid `[bed_mesh]` section.

Example starting configuration:

```ini
[bed_mesh]
algorithm: bicubic
speed: 200
mesh_min: 20,20
mesh_max: 319,297
probe_count: 10,10
```

For first setup, use a conservative mesh speed such as `200`.

After the machine is confirmed stable, higher speeds can be tested.

---

## 9. Rev H accelerometer configuration

Beacon Rev H includes an accelerometer.

Add or verify:

```ini
[resonance_tester]
accel_chip: beacon
probe_points: 90, 90, 20
```

Adjust `probe_points` for your machine if needed.

---

## 10. Restart Klipper

Restart Klipper from Mainsail or run:

```bash
sudo service klipper restart
```

If you get an error, fix config issues before continuing.

---

## 11. PID tune after fresh flash

After a fresh firmware setup, PID tune the hotend and bed.

Hotend example:

```gcode
PID_CALIBRATE HEATER=extruder TARGET=200
```

Bed example:

```gcode
PID_CALIBRATE HEATER=heater_bed TARGET=65
```

Then save:

```gcode
SAVE_CONFIG
```

---

## 12. Heat soak before final Beacon calibration

For the first test, Beacon can be calibrated cold.

For better real-world performance, heat the bed to your normal printing temperature and let the machine stabilize before final calibration.

Example:

```gcode
M140 S65
```

Wait around 20–30 minutes with the bed at temperature before final calibration.

---

## 13. Beacon calibration

Home X and Y:

```gcode
G28 X Y
```

Move to the bed center:

```gcode
G0 X159.5 Y151.5
```

Start Beacon calibration:

```gcode
BEACON_CALIBRATE
```

Use the paper test to set the nozzle height.

While the Beacon calibration manual probe is active, use commands such as:

```gcode
TESTZ Z=-0.01
```

or:

```gcode
TESTZ Z=0.01
```

When the paper has light drag, accept:

```gcode
ACCEPT
```

Important:

* `TESTZ` only works while the manual calibration is active.
* If `TESTZ` says “Unknown command”, restart the calibration process with `BEACON_CALIBRATE`.
* Do not squeeze the paper so hard that it cannot move.
* Aim for light, repeatable drag.

Then save if prompted:

```gcode
SAVE_CONFIG
```

---

## 14. Home and verify Beacon

After calibration, home the printer:

```gcode
G28
```

If `G28 Z` alone causes issues, use full homing:

```gcode
G28 X Y Z
```

Then run:

```gcode
PROBE_ACCURACY
```

Good repeatability should show a very small range and standard deviation.

Example result:

```text
range 0.001445
standard deviation 0.000185
```

---

## 15. Estimate Z backlash

Run:

```gcode
BEACON_ESTIMATE_BACKLASH
```

Example result:

```text
Median distance moving up 1.99880, down 2.00676, delta 0.00796 over 20 samples
```

Record the result.

A small value under 0.01 mm is usually not an immediate failure, but it is useful to know.

---

## 16. Run bed mesh

Run:

```gcode
BED_MESH_CALIBRATE
```

When complete, save:

```gcode
SAVE_CONFIG
```

---

## 17. First layer test and live Z adjustment

Print a simple first layer test.

If the nozzle is too low or dragging, raise the Z offset during the print.

If the nozzle is too high, lower the Z offset during the print.

After the first layer is correct, apply the live Z adjustment to the probe offset:

```gcode
Z_OFFSET_APPLY_PROBE
```
or directly in the Dashboard in the Toolhead Widget the Z-Offset section:
might show like 

Z-Offset: 0.160 

Then save:

Which is your offset you have manually adjusted in the first layer test print. To the
right in the same line you will see the button SAVE

Or in the console type:

```gcode
SAVE_CONFIG
```

Important: only apply the offset once you are happy with the first layer.

---

## 18. Beacon Rev H accelerometer test

Check that Beacon’s accelerometer responds:

```gcode
ACCELEROMETER_QUERY
```

Then check noise:

```gcode
MEASURE_AXES_NOISE
```

If these commands work, continue to resonance testing.

---

## 19. Input shaper calibration

Home first:

```gcode
G28 X Y Z
```
Run the full automatic input shaper calibration:

```gcode
SHAPER_CALIBRATE
```

Or do individual Axis calibration:

```gcode
SHAPER_CALIBRATE AXIS=X
```

```gcode
SHAPER_CALIBRATE AXIS=Y
```

Optional individual axis tests:

```gcode
TEST_RESONANCES AXIS=X
```

```gcode
TEST_RESONANCES AXIS=Y
```

Then SAVE: 
Note: - If you want the data 
      - Before saving Go to section 20 
      - As the temp files will be deleted at boot after the SAVE_CONFIG.

```gcode
SAVE_CONFIG
```

---

## 20. Process and download resonance data from the Raspberry Pi

We have to process the data first:

Full automatic input shaper calibration here if individual axis go down to the next section.

Convert the CSV data to png after input shaping:
If we have done the full automatic input shaper calibration:

```bash
~/klipper/scripts/calibrate_shaper.py /tmp/calibration_data_x_*.csv -o /tmp/shaper_calibrate_x.png
~/klipper/scripts/calibrate_shaper.py /tmp/calibration_data_y_*.csv -o /tmp/shaper_calibrate_y.png
```

All files in the folder:

```bash
ls -lh /tmp
```

OR for only these files:

```bash
ls -lh /tmp | grep -E "calibration_data|shaper_calibrate"
```

You should see something like:

```text
-rw-r--r-- 1 pi   pi   9.6K Jun 10 16:59 calibration_data_x_....csv
-rw-r--r-- 1 pi   pi   9.6K Jun 10 16:59 calibration_data_y_....csv
-rw-rw-r-- 1 pi   pi   130K Jun 10 17:00 shaper_calibrate_x.png
-rw-rw-r-- 1 pi   pi   140K Jun 10 17:00 shaper_calibrate_y.png
```

From a Mac Terminal window, not inside SSH: (When run full calibration)

```bash
scp 'pi@vzbot330.local:/tmp/calibration_data_*.csv' ~/Downloads/
scp 'pi@vzbot330.local:/tmp/shaper_calibrate_*.png' ~/Downloads/
```

---

When individually done shaper calibrate X and/or Y
Convert the CSV data to png after input shaping:
In our Terminal SSH:

```bash
~/klipper/scripts/calibrate_shaper.py /tmp/calibration_data_x_*.csv -o /tmp/shaper_calibrate_x.png
~/klipper/scripts/calibrate_shaper.py /tmp/calibration_data_y_*.csv -o /tmp/shaper_calibrate_y.png
